//! Zamani Quantum Noise (ZQN) — Shared Test Fixtures
//!
//! # Purpose
//!
//! This module provides deterministic, reusable semantic fixtures for the ZQN
//! test suite.
//!
//! Fixtures are intentionally kept below concrete ZQN implementation types.
//! They describe stable test inputs which can be consumed by:
//!
//! - probability tests;
//! - channel tests;
//! - fault tests;
//! - noise-model tests;
//! - calibration tests;
//! - characterization tests;
//! - simulation tests;
//! - propagation tests;
//! - target-compatibility tests;
//! - serialization tests;
//! - determinism tests;
//! - scaling tests;
//! - differential tests;
//! - integration tests.
//!
//! # Architectural rule
//!
//! A fixture is TEST DATA, not production quantum semantics.
//!
//! This file MUST NOT become:
//!
//! - a second noise model implementation;
//! - a second quantum IR;
//! - a simulator;
//! - a random-number generator;
//! - a channel implementation;
//! - a calibration registry;
//! - a hardware registry;
//! - a global mutable test database.
//!
//! Concrete ZQN implementations consume the fixture values.
//!
//! # Canonical qubit identity
//!
//! Whenever a fixture identifies a quantum resource, it uses the canonical:
//!
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::ir::qubit::QubitId
//! crate::quantum::ir::qubit::PhysicalQubitId
//! ```
//!
//! This file deliberately does NOT define a fixture-specific QubitId.
//!
//! This follows the repository-wide rule that ZQN does not own quantum-resource
//! identity. ZQN owns ZQN-domain object IDs through `core::ids`.
//!
//! # Write once, scale everywhere
//!
//! Fixtures must not encode an architectural maximum such as:
//!
//! ```text
//! MAX_QUBITS
//! MAX_SHOTS
//! MAX_FAULTS
//! MAX_OPERATIONS
//! ```
//!
//! A fixture plan describes a requested size and exposes a lazy iterator.
//!
//! Materialization occurs only when a particular test explicitly requests it.
//!
//! Therefore:
//!
//! ```text
//! tiny test       -> tiny materialization
//! medium test     -> medium materialization
//! large test      -> streaming iteration
//! distributed     -> partitioned iteration
//! ```
//!
//! The fixture system itself does not impose a semantic machine-size limit.
//!
//! The actual host/resource limits remain the responsibility of the test,
//! runtime, allocator, and CI environment.
//!
//! # Determinism
//!
//! Fixtures are deterministic.
//!
//! They do not use:
//!
//! - thread-local RNGs;
//! - global RNGs;
//! - system time;
//! - process IDs;
//! - memory addresses;
//! - hash-map iteration order;
//! - filesystem ordering.
//!
//! A `FixtureSeed` is an explicit value used only where deterministic derived
//! values are useful.
//!
//! # Parallel execution
//!
//! A fixture generated with the same seed, scale and index must be identical
//! regardless of whether tests consume it sequentially or concurrently.
//!
//! No mutable global fixture state exists.
//!
//! # Serialization
//!
//! This file does not define the ZQN wire format.
//!
//! Serialization tests should convert fixture data into the canonical ZQN
//! serialization types and test those serializers there.
//!
//! The fixture structures themselves are deliberately simple and stable so
//! they can also be used as input to serialization tests without becoming the
//! serialization contract.
//!
//! # Integration
//!
//! `tests/mod.rs` should expose this module:
//
//! ```text
//! pub(crate) mod fixtures;
//! ```
//!
//! Other ZQN test modules consume it through:
//
//! ```text
//! super::fixtures::*
//! ```
//!
//! or:
//
//! ```text
//! crate::quantum::zqn::tests::fixtures::*
//! ```
//!
//! depending on how the test module tree is composed.
//!
//! The fixture module should remain independent of concrete downstream ZQN
//! modules. This allows the fixture contract to be completed before the rest
//! of ZQN is finished.
//!
//! # Dependency direction
//!
//! ```text
//! quantum::ir::qubit
//!          │
//!          ▼
//!     tests/fixtures
//!          │
//!     ┌────┼─────────────┬──────────────┐
//!     ▼    ▼             ▼              ▼
//! channel noise      simulation    serialization
//! tests  tests        tests           tests
//! ```
//!
//! The fixture layer must never depend on those consumers.
//!
//! # Security
//!
//! Fixture construction must not silently allocate memory proportional to an
//! untrusted size merely because a test requested a fixture plan.
//!
//! Lazy iteration is therefore the default.
//!
//! Explicit materialization is caller-controlled.
//!
//! # Safety
//!
//! This file contains no unsafe Rust.
//!
//! `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` makes accidental introduction of unsafe code a
//! compile-time error.
//!
//! # Rust compatibility
//!
//! Target:
//!
//! - Rust 1.97;
//! - Rust 1.97.1;
//! - Rust 2021 edition;
//! - stable Rust;
//! - no nightly features;
//! - no unsafe code;
//! - no additional dependencies.
//!
//! # Definition of done
//!
//! This file is complete when:
//!
//! 1. all fixtures are deterministic;
//! 2. no global mutable state exists;
//! 3. no hard-coded machine-size ceiling exists;
//! 4. quantum resources use canonical IR IDs;
//! 5. ZQN object identities use `core::ids`;
//! 6. fixture generation can stream rather than materialize;
//! 7. invalid numeric fixture parameters are rejected;
//! 8. scale is represented as data;
//! 9. concrete ZQN implementations are not duplicated here;
//! 10. tests can use the fixtures without modifying this file;
//! 11. the file compiles on Rust 1.97/1.97.1;
//! 12. `unsafe` is forbidden.

